            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"The Church as the spiritual people of God or the New Israel, encompassing all believers in Christ, both Jew and Gentile.\",\"primary_basis\": [\"Galatians 6:16\",\"Romans 9:6-8\",\"Romans 2:28-29\",\"1 Peter 2:9\"],\"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"Ethnic, national Israel, maintaining a distinct covenantal identity and future prophetic destiny entirely separate from the Church.\",\"basis\": [\"Romans 11:25-27\",\"Revelation 7:4-8\",\"Jeremiah 31:35-37\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"},{\"meaning\": \"Jesus Christ Himself as the true and faithful Israelite who perfectly fulfills the covenantal vocation of the nation.\",\"basis\": [\"Matthew 2:15\",\"Isaiah 49:3\",\"John 15:1\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\": false,\"confidence\": \"high\",\"notes\": \"Historically, Covenant Theology, Catholicism, and Orthodoxy have viewed the Church as the spiritual continuation of Israel. However, Dispensationalism strongly insists on a literal, ethnic interpretation of Israel distinct from the Church, making the interpretation heavily dependent on one's theological framework.\"}",
